Mirrored pose blending read a dead stack slot, which is why characters float.

cPoseAccumulator::BlendTrans handles a mirrored animation by rewriting the
incoming translation into a local, `vtemp`, and repointing `pTrans` at it. The
local was declared *inside* the `if (bMirror)` block, but `pTrans` is
dereferenced after that block closes -- so every mirrored blend read a slot
whose lifetime had ended. BlendRot, right above it, declares its `qtemp`
outside the block and is correct; this is the one that drifted.

MWCC left the slot untouched and the retail game worked by accident. GCC reuses
it, so the mirrored path blended whatever the compiler had put there since.
On limb nodes that is a small wrong offset; on the animated root node, whose
translation is the character's hip height, it is the whole skeleton's height --
the character lifts off the pitch while m_v3Position, which the ball and the
gameplay code follow, stays on it. That is the "ball trails behind a floating
carrier" shape exactly.

Measured before the fix: with the root node's blend inputs all reading ~0.29
from the animations, the accumulator held 0.84, and the posed feet sat ~0.87
above the pitch for hundreds of frames at a time. Mirrored controllers were
active in every occurrence. After the fix a two-minute match logs no sustained
foot lift at all.

Because it is undefined behaviour rather than bad data, it is sensitive to
codegen: adding unrelated code to cSAnim::BlendTrans made the symptom vanish
--- a/src/Game/PoseAccumulator.cpp
+++ b/src/Game/PoseAccumulator.cpp
@@ -393,11 +393,18 @@
